WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:05.850
In 1986, Castlevania took players inside Dracula's
sprawling, horrifying mansion, and gamers

00:00:05.850 --> 00:00:07.129
never looked back.

00:00:07.129 --> 00:00:11.781
Now, over three decades later, Castlevania
isn't just a major video game series, it's

00:00:11.781 --> 00:00:16.990
also an animated series, a series of comic
books, and the origin of an entire video game

00:00:16.990 --> 00:00:17.990
genre.

00:00:17.990 --> 00:00:21.449
And just like Vlad's castle, Castlevania is
full of secrets.

00:00:21.449 --> 00:00:25.099
There's much more to the game than whips and
vampires.

00:00:25.099 --> 00:00:26.439
Mysterious origins

00:00:26.439 --> 00:00:30.360
Over 30 official Castlevania games have been
published, and a few are even considered to

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:34.200
be some of the greatest games of all time,
so there's no doubt that Castlevania is an

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:35.250
important franchise.

00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:39.630
And yet, historians have no clue who actually
created the thing.

00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:43.900
Koji Igarashi, Castlevania's longtime producer
and the name most commonly associated with

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:49.210
the franchise, didn't start working on the
series until 1997's Symphony of the Night.

00:00:49.210 --> 00:00:52.690
Castlevania doesn't have a "real" credit sequence,
ending with a tribute to people like "Christopher

00:00:52.690 --> 00:00:54.830
Bee" and "Boris Karloffice."

00:00:54.830 --> 00:00:56.500
Why don't we know who made this game?

00:00:56.500 --> 00:01:01.100
In the '80s and early '90s, Japanese game
developers used pseudonyms at the behest of

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:05.650
their bosses in order to make it harder for
rival companies to poach talented staff members.

00:01:05.650 --> 00:01:09.930
Some historians have tried to track down Castlevania's
creator, but haven't gotten much further than

00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:14.590
the name "Hitoshi Akamatsu", but other than
a handful of credits on other Konami games,

00:01:14.590 --> 00:01:15.970
nobody seems to know much about him.

00:01:15.970 --> 00:01:20.580
From the looks of things, Mr. Akamatsu exited
the game industry in the '90s and disappeared

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:22.680
— if he ever existed at all.

00:01:25.180 --> 00:01:26.979
Beyond Nintendo

00:01:26.979 --> 00:01:31.090
The first three numbered Castlevania games
launched on the Nintendo Entertainment System,

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:35.299
leading to the impression that the series
started as an NES-exclusive franchise… except

00:01:35.299 --> 00:01:36.470
it didn't.

00:01:36.470 --> 00:01:40.440
Only a month after the first game debuted,
the second entry in the series, Vampire Killer,

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:46.100
arrived on Microsoft's MSX computer as a Japan
exclusive, and Nintendo also ran Castlevania

00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:48.500
in some of their Versus System arcade cabinets.

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:53.000
There's also Haunted Castle, Castlevania's
first original arcade outing, which is regarded

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:57.160
as a pretty terrible, incredibly difficult
game with awful controls.

00:01:57.160 --> 00:02:01.241
Even worse, Simon Belmont only has one life,
and you can only continue three times, no

00:02:01.241 --> 00:02:03.650
matter how many quarters you pump into the
machine.

00:02:03.650 --> 00:02:07.530
It wasn't long before the Belmonts and friends
broke out onto every system out there, — but

00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:11.910
even early on, Nintendo wasn't the franchise's
exclusive distributor.

00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:14.120
Bram Stoker's Dracula

00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:18.390
It's pretty obvious that Castlevania owes
quite a bit to Bram Stoker's vampire magnum

00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:20.450
opus, but just how much?

00:02:20.450 --> 00:02:24.280
Konami made Stoker's book part of the official
timeline in Castlevania: Bloodlines.

00:02:24.280 --> 00:02:28.470
According to Konami, the book's Quincey Morris,
who helps Jonathan Harker kill Dracula before

00:02:28.470 --> 00:02:32.870
succumbing to his vampire-induced wounds,
is actually a member of the Belmont line.

00:02:32.870 --> 00:02:37.200
In the game series, Quincy dispatched Dracula
and went on to father Bloodlines' hero John

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:38.200
Morris.

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:41.420
Jonathan, from Castlevania: Portrait of Ruin,
is Quincy's grandson.

00:02:41.420 --> 00:02:42.880
It's not a perfect fit, however.

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:47.100
According to Bloodlines, John saw Quincy murder
Dracula when he was a kid, and without Harker's

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:48.100
help.

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:51.720
As a storytelling device, linking Stoker and
Castlevania raises more questions than it

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:52.720
answers.

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:55.380
As a tribute to Castlevania's primary inspiration,
however?

00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:57.130
It works just fine.

00:02:57.130 --> 00:02:58.500
Alucard's origins

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:03.520
One of the most popular characters in Castlevania
is Alucard, Dracula's half-vampire son, and

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:07.940
the hero of Castlevania: Symphony of the Night,
a noble and fierce warrior who's put his dad

00:03:07.940 --> 00:03:11.020
back in the ground and saved humanity multiple
times.

00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:14.780
While everything about his backstory reeks
of classic video games, Alucard, like his

00:03:14.780 --> 00:03:17.690
old man, is actually lifted from older source
material.

00:03:17.690 --> 00:03:23.570
While not as well known as 1931's Dracula,
the film's second sequel, 1943's Son of Dracula,

00:03:23.570 --> 00:03:25.660
is actually responsible for Alucard.

00:03:25.660 --> 00:03:30.410
In the film, horror star Lon Chaney Jr. plays
the mysterious Count Alucard, who seduces

00:03:30.410 --> 00:03:33.129
a young heiress with promises of immortality.

00:03:33.129 --> 00:03:37.740
Of course, 'Alucard' is just 'Dracula' spelled
backwards… so even though 1943 wasn't great

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:40.480
at being subtle, the name stuck.

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:41.620
Animated adventures

00:03:41.620 --> 00:03:46.530
Netflix's Castlevania is the first video-game-to-film
adaptation to earn a "fresh" score on Rotten

00:03:46.530 --> 00:03:48.790
Tomatoes, with a rating of 90 percent.

00:03:48.790 --> 00:03:51.349
But it's not even the first Castlevania-themed
cartoon.

00:03:51.349 --> 00:03:56.909
In 1989, DIC Entertainment produced a Saturday
morning cartoon called Captain N: The Game

00:03:56.909 --> 00:03:59.930
Master, and characters from Castlevania played
a major role.

00:03:59.930 --> 00:04:04.379
Captain N tells the story of a teenager named
Kevin Keene who gets sucked into his Nintendo

00:04:04.379 --> 00:04:08.550
and faces off against villains from various
8-bit video games, including deranged versions

00:04:08.550 --> 00:04:11.599
of Punch-Out's King Hippo and Metroid's Mother
Brain.

00:04:11.599 --> 00:04:17.000
Along the way, Kevin gets help from Mega Man,
Kid Icarus, a sentient Game Boy, and Castlevania's

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:19.150
first leading man, Simon Belmont.

00:04:19.150 --> 00:04:23.150
"I'm Simon Belmont, vampire hunter, at your service."

00:04:23.150 --> 00:04:27.620
In Captain N, Simon is a vain adventurer who
cares more about his dashing good looks than

00:04:27.620 --> 00:04:31.840
fighting evil... which is a pretty unfaithful
adaptation, as far as cartoons go.

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:36.750
Dracula shows up as a recurring villain, and
Alucard shows up in the third season episode

00:04:36.750 --> 00:04:41.780
"Return to Castlevania," as a wisecracking
teenager with sunglasses, a bat-shaped skateboard,

00:04:41.780 --> 00:04:44.860
and an unhealthy obsession with rock and roll.

00:04:44.860 --> 00:04:49.560
"Yo pops! Check out my new bat flip!"

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:57.720
It was pretty much the most cringeworthy early-'90s
thing ever, and thankfully, that version of

00:04:57.720 --> 00:04:59.980
Alucard quickly disappeared and never returned.

00:04:59.980 --> 00:05:01.300
"Alright!

00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:02.860
Vampires rule!"

00:05:04.060 --> 00:05:05.220
Timelines

00:05:05.560 --> 00:05:09.340
You don't make almost 20 games in ten years
without making a few mistakes, but there's

00:05:09.350 --> 00:05:13.660
one decision in particular that Koji Igarashi
wishes that he could take back.

00:05:13.660 --> 00:05:17.830
While developing Symphony of the Night, Igarashi
developed a series timeline for the developers

00:05:17.830 --> 00:05:20.090
to use as an internal reference.

00:05:20.090 --> 00:05:24.410
Once it leaked to a gaming magazine, the Castlevania
team decided to make it official.

00:05:24.410 --> 00:05:29.620
According to the timeline, Dracula returns
every 100 years, but unfortunately, the official

00:05:29.620 --> 00:05:34.169
timeline is so thorough that Igarashi said
that it hurt his creative process down the

00:05:34.169 --> 00:05:35.350
line, explaining,

00:05:35.350 --> 00:05:39.949
"...since Dracula only appears every 100 years,
we made the whole timeline and ran out of

00:05:39.949 --> 00:05:42.150
places to put in another game."

00:05:42.150 --> 00:05:47.070
Of course, when continuity gets too crowded,
you can just throw it all out and start over.

00:05:47.070 --> 00:05:51.510
That's what Konami did in 2010 when it handed
a new developer the reins to Castlevania:

00:05:51.510 --> 00:05:56.350
Lords of Shadow, which takes place in an entirely
different universe… but that game almost

00:05:56.350 --> 00:05:57.990
didn't happen at all.

00:05:57.990 --> 00:05:59.720
Metal Gear Shadow

00:05:59.720 --> 00:06:05.040
In the late 2000s, Konami enlisted MercurySteam
to create a brand new, 3D reboot of the Castlevania

00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:06.040
franchise.

00:06:06.040 --> 00:06:09.241
The developers put together a prototype and
showed it to Konami's executives, but they

00:06:09.241 --> 00:06:13.330
grew nervous about all the changes that were
being made to their classic franchise, so

00:06:13.330 --> 00:06:16.920
they stripped away the Castlevania branding
and allowed development to continue.

00:06:16.920 --> 00:06:20.289
Lords of Shadow made its public debut at Gamescom
2009.

00:06:20.289 --> 00:06:24.610
Later that year, MercurySteam showed the latest
version of Lords of Shadow to Konami higher-ups,

00:06:24.610 --> 00:06:28.120
which included Metal Gear Solid overlord Hideo
Kojima.

00:06:28.120 --> 00:06:31.570
Kojima loved it and asked the room, "Why isn't
this Castlevania?"

00:06:31.570 --> 00:06:35.380
Eventually, Kojima joined Lords of Shadow
as a producer, and though he'd later leave

00:06:35.380 --> 00:06:40.060
Konami, Kojima is uniquely responsible for
the continuation of Castlevania as we know

00:06:40.060 --> 00:06:41.060
it.

00:06:41.320 --> 00:06:42.320
Thanks for watching!

00:06:42.320 --> 00:06:44.229
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:44.229 --> 00:06:46.320
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

